NAME
       man - read the manual pages

SYNOPSIS
       man [SECTION] PAGE...
       man -k KEYWORD
       man -f PAGE

DESCRIPTION
       man shows the manual page of a command, a configuration file or a
       concept. Manual pages are the built-in reference of RHEL, and they
       are available during the RHCSA exam, so practise finding things in
       them.

       A page is split into sections with standard headings: NAME (one line
       saying what it is), SYNOPSIS (how to call it: [brackets] mark
       optional parts, | separates choices, ... means "can repeat"),
       DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS, EXAMPLES, FILES and SEE ALSO (related pages).

       The pages are grouped into numbered sections. The same name can exist
       in several, so give the section when it matters (man 5 passwd is the
       file, man 1 passwd the command):

       1  user commands             5  file formats (fstab, passwd)
       2  system calls              7  overviews (selinux, regex)
       3  library functions         8  administration commands
       4  devices

       In SEE ALSO and elsewhere, a page is written as name(section), such
       as fstab(5).

       The pager shows the page one screen at a time. Inside it: Space or
       PgDn next page, b previous page, /WORD search forward, n next match,
       N previous match, g start, G end, q quit.

OPTIONS
       -k KEYWORD
              Search the NAME lines of all pages for KEYWORD and list the
              matches with their sections (the same as apropos). Good when
              you do not know which command does a job: man -k partition.

       -f PAGE
              Show the NAME line of PAGE in each section (the same as
              whatis).

EXAMPLES
       Read the page about the fstab file format:

           man 5 fstab

       Find the commands that deal with logical volumes:

           man -k 'logical volume'

       Find the right section for a word:

           man -f passwd

       Look for examples quickly: open the page, type /EXAMPLE and press
       Enter.

NOTES
       If man -k finds nothing for a word that should exist, the search
       index may be old: run mandb as root to rebuild it.

       Many packages also install longer documents in /usr/share/doc/PACKAGE
       (for example sample configuration files). info pages exist for some
       GNU tools (info coreutils).

FILES
       /usr/share/man
              Where the pages are kept.

SEE ALSO
       apropos(1), whatis(1), mandb(8), info(1), less(1)
